<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Permesta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Universal Struggle Charter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Permesta was a regional rebellion in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, named after the Universal Struggle Charter proclaimed by army officers in Makassar in March 1957. Based mainly in North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, its leaders demanded greater regional autonomy and a fairer share of export revenue, and in 1958 they joined the PRRI rebels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. Despite covert American support, the movement was defeated by central government forces, and its last fighters surrendered in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/permesta.jpg" alt="Permesta" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JJUPLOADS22 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Permesta_flag_minahasa_reconstruction.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Permesta was founded on 2 March 1957 by civil and military leaders of Eastern Indonesia. Its name is a syllabic abbreviation of the Indonesian Perjuangan Semesta. The movement first had its centre in Makassar, then the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, but as support in South Sulawesi faded its headquarters moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manado">Manado</a> in North Sulawesi. Its leaders included Ventje Sumual, Alexander Evert Kawilarang and Joop Warouw.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 17 February 1958 Permesta joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prri-rebellion">Revolutionary Government of the Republic of Indonesia</a>, the PRRI, which had been proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> two days earlier. The CIA supplied an air arm, the Revolutionary Air Force or AUREV, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manado">Manado</a>, with 15 B-26 Invader bombers, some P-51 Mustang fighters, pilots, mechanics, weapons and spare parts. One of the rebel leaders, Lukas J. Palar, was killed in an air strike on 10 March 1958.</p><p>After government forces broke the PRRI in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> the fighting moved east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manado">Manado</a> fell at the end of June 1958. The rebels fought on as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> organised in area commands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>. They also issued stamps, used at Kotamobagu and Inobonto from March to August 1959, and printed banknotes in 1958 and 1959.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The movement turned on itself. In April 1960 a unit under Jan Timbuleng captured Warouw, who usually mediated between Kawilarang and Sumual, and killed him on 15 October 1960; his remains were found only in 1992 near Tombatu and reburied in his home town of Remboken. Sumual in turn had Timbuleng detained and executed.</p><p>Kawilarang surrendered on 14 April 1961 and Sumual on 20 October 1961, and the remaining fighters were given amnesty. The conflict cost the lives of 4,000 government soldiers and 2,000 rebels. Jan Willem Gerungan, the commander in Central Sulawesi, went over to the insurgency of Kahar Muzakkar and was arrested and executed in 1965.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Permesta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1950s it suppressed regional rebellions, including the Darul Islam movement, which sought an Islamic state, and the PRRI and <b>Permesta</b> revolts in Sumatra and Sulawesi in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
